{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Kernighan-Lin Partitioning.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Kernighan-Lin Partitioning.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"kernighan lin partitioning kernighan lin partitioning","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Kernighan-Lin Partitioning.md","fileName":"Kernighan-Lin Partitioning.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Kernighan-Lin Partitioning","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-14 21:34\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Network and Uncertain Data Clustering]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Network and Uncertain Data Clustering","dataclustering.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Kernighan-Lin Partitioning.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Kernighan-Lin Partitioning.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"kernighan lin partitioning kernighan lin partitioning","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Kernighan-Lin Partitioning.md","fileName":"Kernighan-Lin Partitioning.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Kernighan-Lin Partitioning","heading":"Kernighan-Lin Partitioning","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Kernighan-Lin partitioning improves an initial balanced two-way graph division by considering paired vertex swaps. During a pass it selects swaps for their cumulative gain, locks moved vertices, and then keeps the best improving prefix.\n\nAllowing temporary negative-gain moves helps escape some local traps that greedy improvement cannot cross. The result remains dependent on initialization and is commonly embedded in multilevel or repeated schemes.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Network and Uncertain Data Clustering","dataclustering.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Kernighan-Lin Partitioning.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Kernighan-Lin Partitioning.md","sourceType":"markdo